RDPServer 0.2-rc2 (release candidate)
=====================================

Lets you use this RISC OS desktop from another computer with Remote
Desktop: Windows' Remote Desktop Connection (mstsc), Microsoft's Windows
App on macOS, iOS and Android, FreeRDP or Remmina on Linux, and
!RDPClient on another RISC OS machine.

FOR YOUR OWN NETWORK. Clients that ask for it (all the usual ones do)
get TLS 1.2, as from a Windows server; older clients get RDP's own
encryption (RC4), which is weak. Keep it behind your router, never make
port 3389 reachable from the internet, and set a login (below) before
you use it.

Needs: SharedUnixLibrary, ARMEABISupport and CryptRandom (PackMan). Any
desktop mode can be shared: 2, 4, 16 or 256 colours, 4096, thousands
or millions (16 million is the quickest).

Using it
--------
Double-click !RDPServer. Its icon appears on the icon bar, and it
listens on port 3389. The first time, it makes this computer's key
(stored in Choices:RDPServer); that can take a minute, with the
hourglass showing. The icon changes while a client is connected.

  Select on the icon   the Status window: what it's doing; the client
                       (its address and computer name), the user, how
                       long it's been connected, its RDP version, the
                       security (TLS and cipher, or RC4), the screen
                       mode and the client's colour depth, keep-alive,
                       sound and what's been sent; and the last few
                       messages
  Adjust on the icon   Choices
  Menu on the icon     Info, Status..., Choices..., Disconnect, Quit

It uses almost no time while nothing is happening; while a client is
connected it looks for changes on the screen twenty times a second.

When the desktop shuts down, the server carries on (with no icon on the
icon bar), so a client stays connected and can click Restart in the
box that follows. Quitting !RDPServer itself stops it. When the computer
restarts, the client is shown "RISC OS is restarting" and the connection
is dropped as if the network had gone (once the desktop has shut down, a
click or Return from the client sends that page first, then the click: it
may be the Restart button; if no restart follows within 5 s, the client
can connect again): a client that reconnects by
itself (Windows App, Remote Desktop Connection, FreeRDP with
/auto-reconnect) connects again once the computer is back, if !RDPServer
starts with it (add it to Boot's Run list in !Configure).

The server itself is a module (RDPSMod, which !Run loads and Quit
removes), so it goes on working when the desktop doesn't multitask:
while an error box is open, the client still sees the screen (the box
included) and can click the box's buttons; a program that keeps the
machine to itself doesn't stop it either. If such a program is stopped
with Alt-Break while the server is in the middle of its turn, the server
starts again by itself, and a client that supports automatic
reconnection should connect again by itself within a few seconds,
without asking for the password (seen with Windows App, FreeRDP's
/auto-reconnect and rdesktop). It never holds the desktop up
for more than a couple of centiseconds at a time, also while a client is
connecting.

Disconnect (on the icon's menu) drops the client at once, even one that
is still connecting.

There is one client at a time, as RISC OS has one desktop and one user:
while a client is connected, another is turned away (the Log says so).
A client that connects but doesn't finish connecting within 30 seconds
is dropped, so it can't keep others out.

Setting a login
---------------
Until you set one, anyone who can reach the port can connect. In
Choices, type a user name and a password and click Save (or press
Return). Clients must then give them. To change the password, type a new
one; to let anyone connect again, click Clear login (it takes effect
straight away). The password isn't stored, only a salted SHA-256 of it
(Choices:RDPServer.logins).

A client that doesn't send the user name and password (Windows' Remote
Desktop Connection, say, which doesn't ask for them for this kind of
server), or sends a wrong one, gets a login screen instead of the
desktop: nothing of the RISC OS screen is shown, and what's typed there
goes nowhere else. Type the user name and password and press Return (or
click Log in). It types with this computer's keyboard layout (UK, USA,
Germany, France and the Netherlands are known; for others, the client's
layout if it's one of those, else USA; the Log says which), and accent
keys that wait for the next key don't work there. After a wrong
password it waits 3 seconds; after 5, or 2 minutes without logging in,
the client is disconnected, and new connections are turned away for 3
seconds.

Choices also sets the port. Changing it disconnects a connected client.

Sound
-----
What this computer plays can go to the client too: tick "Send this
computer's sound to the client" in Choices (it's off to start with, also
after updating from an earlier version that sent it without asking). The
client must ask for sound as well (Windows App, Remote Desktop Connection
and FreeRDP do by default; rdesktop only with -r sound). Turning it off
stops the sound at once; turning it on takes effect from the next client
(also for the one connected, if it had sound and Choices stopped it).
It's sent as it is, 16-bit stereo at the
computer's own sample rate (about 1.5 Mbit/s at 48 kHz), so it suits a
home network better than the internet. It reaches the client about a
third of a second late.

The computer goes on playing it too, unless you also tick "Mute this
computer while a client listens" in Choices: it's then silent while a client
listens, and plays again when the client goes. The choice takes effect
at once, also while a client is connected.

If a program changes the sample rate while a client is listening, the
sound to that client stops until it connects again.

Connecting
----------
On the other computer, connect to this computer's IP address:
  Windows:   mstsc /v:192.168.1.50
  FreeRDP:   xfreerdp3 /v:192.168.1.50
  RDPClient: as for any server

The first time, the client says it can't verify this computer's
certificate. That's expected: RDPServer makes its own (it isn't signed by
anyone the client knows). Before you accept it, check that it's this
computer's: the client can show the certificate's fingerprint (Windows
calls it the thumbprint), and RDPServer's Status window and Log show it
when it starts ("TLS on: certificate SHA-1 ..." and "SHA-256 ..."). If
they match, accept it and tick "don't ask again"; from then on the client
warns only if something else answers in this computer's place.

To refuse clients that don't use TLS, tick "Only allow TLS connections"
in Choices (from the next client on). A refused client is told the
server requires TLS, and the Log says "refused: it didn't ask for TLS".
Leave it off if you use an old client that can't do TLS 1.2 (an
unpatched Windows 7, say).

The certificate is made once, from this computer's key, as
Choices:RDPServer.cert.pem and key.pem. Delete both and RDPServer makes
a new one (clients then ask again). With neither, or if a client
doesn't ask for TLS, the connection uses RC4; the Log says which
("security: ...").

The client gets the RISC OS screen at its own size. Mouse: left is
Select, middle is Menu, right is Adjust; the scroll wheel works where a
local USB mouse's wheel does. The keyboard works by key position, with
RISC OS's keyboard layout. Some clients send some or all characters as
the characters themselves instead (Windows App on a Mac, on-screen
keyboards, input methods): those are typed as they are, if RISC OS's
alphabet has them. Keys go to whichever window has the input focus on
RISC OS.

If the connection is lost
--------------------------
One client at a time can be connected. If a client goes without closing
its connection (that computer went to sleep, or off the network), a new
connection is held for a few seconds while RDPServer checks the one
that's there: if it doesn't answer, it's dropped and the new one comes
in; if it does, the new one is refused ("refused ...: the client that's
connected is still there" in the Log). Clients that use TLS and take
"heartbeats" (Windows App, mstsc and FreeRDP do) are also sent one every
5 seconds: if they stop coming (this computer hung, say), the client
warns after 15 seconds and connects again by itself after 30. The Log
says what each client takes ("keep-alive: ..."). rdesktop and RDPClient
take neither, so for them only the check above applies: the whole
screen is sent again, and if none of it is taken the new connection
comes in. Any client that takes nothing for a minute while there's
something waiting to go to it is disconnected (its program has stopped
but its connection hasn't closed, as a RISC OS client's can).

It writes a log, Choices:RDPServer.Log (and libxrdp's own, XrdpLog):
connections, sound, every mouse button and wheel event, and Shift, Ctrl
and Alt. Other keys are logged without saying which, so nothing you type
is kept.
A click goes to RISC OS when the client lets go of the button (or after
a quarter of a second, if you hold it, or as soon as you drag); the
button is then released 6 cs later by a ticker event, so double-clicks
reach the Filer as double-clicks.

What isn't done yet
-------------------
- NLA (Windows' "network level authentication"): a client set to
  require it won't connect. Windows App and mstsc don't by default.
- Only changed areas are sent, but there's no bitmap cache yet, and no
  quick handling of window moves and scrolling.
- The clipboard, and file or printer sharing.
- H.264: video is sent as ordinary screen updates, so it uses a lot of
  the network and isn't smooth at large sizes.
- The pointer shape: the client shows a plain arrow (sent by RDPServer,
  so clients such as RDPClient show one too), not RISC OS's own pointer.
- RDP 4 clients (RDPClient with its "RDP4" option ticked) can't connect.
- Video and 3D shown in a hardware overlay (VideoOverlay, and EGL
  programs such as Reel, ReelEGL and Mesa games) appear on the client as
  a black window: the GPU draws overlays straight to the display, so they
  never reach screen memory where RDPServer reads the picture. They still
  show on this computer's own monitor. While a client is connected
  RDPServer sets EGL$Overlay to "off" (and puts your own setting back
  when the client goes) and RDPServer$Clients to 1, so programs can draw
  the ordinary way instead; Reel and the EGL library don't do that yet.
- Changing the screen mode while a client is connected resizes the
  client's desktop to the new mode; the client redraws its window as it
  does that, so it flashes once. A mode change at the same size (other
  colours, say) doesn't resize it, so doesn't flash. A client that
  can't be resized (it didn't say it could) is disconnected: connect
  again.

If it's slow
------------
The Log says, when RDPServer starts, how fast this computer encrypts
("TLS here: ..."), and every 5 seconds while a client is connected how
much it sent ("... KB/s"). The client's connection asks the network
stack for a 64 KB send buffer; to try another size, *Set
RDPServer$SendBuffer to it in KB (16 to 1024) and connect again (a big
one can use up the stack's buffers: see MbufManager).

RDPServer finds what changed by reading the screen. A pass over it takes
5 cs while things are changing (25 cs once the screen has been still for
2 seconds), however often RDPServer gets a turn. Each pass reads 1 row in
8, a different row each time, and where one of those has changed it reads
the rest of that 64 x 64 area at once: a change 8 rows tall or more is
found in the pass it happens in, and every row is read once in 8 passes.
At start-up the Log says how fast the screen can be read ("screen
read: ..."); the 5-second figures say how long a pass took and how much
was read.

What changed is sent as compressed 64 x 64 bitmaps, and areas of one
colour as fills (a few bytes each). At start-up the Log says how long
this computer takes to encode a whole screen like the middle of the one
it shows ("encoding here: ..."): converting it to the client's colours,
compressing it, and bulk compressing (MPPC, when the client asks for it).
When a client connects, "updates: ..." says which of those it gets.
While a client is connected, anything that holds this computer up for
10 cs or more is logged ("slow: ...").

To try other settings, *Set any of these and start RDPServer again:
  RDPServer$SampleRows   1 row in this many a pass (1, 2, 4, 8 or 16;
                         1 reads every row, as in 0.2-rc1)
  RDPServer$ScanRows     the most rows read a turn (0: no limit)
  RDPServer$ScreenCopy   1 to read the screen a word at a time, 2 eight
                         words at a time (normally the quicker is used)
  RDPServer$Fills        0 to send areas of one colour as bitmaps
  RDPServer$BulkCompress 0 not to bulk compress (MPPC): more to send,
                         less work here
  RDPServer$ClickTime    how long a click holds the mouse button down, in
                         cs (2 to 50; 6)
  RDPServer$Clicks       0 to send mouse buttons down and up just as the
                         client does, not as one click

Reporting problems
------------------
Please say which client you used (and its version), what you did and
what happened, and attach Choices:RDPServer.Log and XrdpLog from that
run (they start afresh each time RDPServer starts). They hold no
passwords or typed text.
  https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-rdpserver/issues

Command line
------------
RDPServer -setlogin NAME PASSWORD
RDPServer -clearlogin
  set the one login clients must give, or let anyone connect (as
  Choices does)

